      "writtenOn": "2026-09-16T06:45:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just want to better understand the issue, e.g, the VA from host is VA1, and we perform the security check for its physical page PA (which is got by __pkvm_pa(VA1), is correct). And passing the check, pKVM is trying to access PA by using VA1. However VA1 is not mapped in pKVM\u0027s mmu as we map all physical addresses with direct mapping except for pKVM\u0027s text/data, which is the reason for us couldn\u0027t make __pkvm_pa always returning VA - PAGE_OFFSET for any VA.\n\nSo a malicious host can pass such VA1 to cause a #PF and panic pKVM, right?",
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "062fa557_61f1c0bb",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-16T08:57:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah exactly, it seems it is guaranteed that this issue will result in a #PF in pKVM, i.e. just a DoS, so it is not a critical issue.",
      "parentUuid": "bab49e01_dc9b13e4",
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a01a5957_48883294",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/memory.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 165,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T13:57:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "just a thought: wondering if we could consider NULL as a special case as a documentation for __pa implementation in kernel?:\n```\n  static inline void *kern_pkvm_va(void *va)\n  {\n  \treturn va ? __pkvm_va(__pkvm_pa(va)) : NULL;\n  }\n```\n\nI was reading the __pa implementation and it calls __phys_addr() which BUGS_ON() if va \u003d\u003d NULL when CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL enabled. But host can genuinely pass va \u003d NULL and we have check in pkvm for NULL like this eg:\n\n```\n        shared_lapic_regs \u003d kern_pkvm_va(READ_ONCE(shared_apic-\u003eregs));\n        if (!shared_lapic_regs) {\n                ret \u003d -EINVAL;\n                goto unshare_apic;\n        }\n```\n\nThis could panic when CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL enabled.\n\nBut we disable CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL in undef.h so not an issue at all for us right now.",
      "fixSuggestions": [
        {
          "fixId": "eee0931f_bb7ce781",
          "description": "prompt_to_edit API",
          "replacements": [
            {
              "path": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/memory.h",
              "range": {
                "startLine": 165,
                "startChar": 0,
                "endLine": 166,
                "endChar": 0
              },
              "replacement": "\treturn va ? __pkvm_va(__pkvm_pa(va)) : NULL;\n"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "385f7538_233ef08d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/memory.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 165,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T21:55:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thinking about this... First, if we do that, we should rather do that for any invalid va below PAGE_OFFSET, not just for NULL? In particular, if the host is able to trick pKVM to access (e.g. write) a VA \u003d\u003d 64K, which is the start of pKVM\u0027s vmemmap, pretty bad things would happen.\n\nNow, thinking about what exactly happens if the host passes such an invalid va, e.g. NULL. __pkvm_pa(va) will return (u64)(-PAGE_OFFSET) which, with 4-level paging, is 0x10000000000000000 - 0xffff888000000000 \u003d 0x778000000000 \u003d 119.5TB which is not gonna be a RAM address with 4-level paging (systems with that much RAM use 5-level paging). So it will not pass is_memory_range() check in pkvm_host_share_hyp/pkvm_host_donate_hyp.\n\nAnd similarly with 5-level paging, __pkvm_pa(va) will be 0x10000000000000000 - 0xff11000000000000 \u003d 0xef000000000000 \u003d 59.75PB which is not gonna be a RAM address on any system.\n\nSo it seems we are safe?",
      "parentUuid": "a01a5957_48883294",
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0c274826_826b5843",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/memory.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 165,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T22:18:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wouldn\u0027t like to require callers of kern_pkvm_va() to check the return value, as that would make it less aligned with pKVM-ARM\u0027s kern_hyp_va() and the code more complicated. If at all, I think we\u0027d rather validate __pkvm_va(phys) inside pkvm_host_share_hyp/pkvm_host_donate_hyp (since pKVM is not supposed to use a VA returned by kern_pkvm_va() until pkvm_host_share_hyp/pkvm_host_donate_hyp succeeded for it, and pKVM already takes care not to do that).\n\nAnd given the above calculations, it seems even that is not required?",
      "parentUuid": "385f7538_233ef08d",
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd154630_ecffbc9b",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/memory.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 165,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-25T01:59:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Now, thinking about what exactly happens if the host passes such an invalid va, e.g. NULL. __pkvm_pa(va) will return (u64)(-PAGE_OFFSET) which, with 4-level paging, is 0x10000000000000000 - 0xffff888000000000 \u003d 0x778000000000 \u003d 119.5TB which is not gonna be a RAM address with 4-level paging (systems with that much RAM use 5-level paging). So it will not pass is_memory_range() check in pkvm_host_share_hyp/pkvm_host_donate_hyp.\n\nyes true, we are safe as we don\u0027t use the va address anywhere directly before vetting it through donation apis and does not pass is_memory_range() in the donation apis\n\nWhat I was referring to was - if CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL is set, it will panic the system for an input that pkvm validates later - hence that panic could be avoided.  But I realized CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL is undefined in pkvm and we would not panic anyways - so the changes is okay as it is.\n\nMy earlier suggestion about NULL as a special case was too narrow as you rightly pointed out. If ever there is a need to validate values in kern_pkvm_va(), it should be the whole range below PAGE_OFFSET, but it is not needed. I was thinking about having it as a documentation in the code - in case if we plan to support CONFIG_DEBUG_VIRTUAL in future.\n\n\u003e I wouldn\u0027t like to require callers of kern_pkvm_va() to check the return value, ...\n\nYou mean removing the existing checks for non-NULL in the code? I think its fine to remove it as the logic would bailout anyways in the donation apis.\n\n\u003e And given the above calculations, it seems even that is not required?\n\nYeah, is_memory_range() already takes care of it and explicit check is not needed",
      "parentUuid": "0c274826_826b5843",
      "revId": "fd944b03fa060150e9209d000ae58ef796e3c0e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
